<!-- @before First Principles :: guide -->
### Use the update law as well as the merge law

For state-based replication, local updates must move upward in the type’s order, merge must compute the join, and information must eventually propagate. Classical operation-based replication has different delivery and duplicate requirements. A counter’s convergence proof does not enforce stock or balance limits. [Shapiro et al.’s CRDT study](https://www.lip6.fr/Marc.Shapiro/papers/2011/Comprehensive-CRDTs-RR7506-2011-01.pdf).

<!-- @before Staff Engineer Thinking :: staff -->
### Review the visible conflict policy

An OR-set’s concurrent add can survive a remove that did not observe it. That is a defined result, not proof that it matches the user’s intent. Decide the intended policy and safe metadata retirement before selecting a library.

<!-- @before Answer Key :: guide -->
### Separate convergence from the product choice

Answer 1 describes classical operation-based requirements; a specific library can provide a different documented delivery contract. In answer 4, add-wins and remove-wins are alternative semantics, not a universal UX ranking. In answer 10, replica convergence does not guarantee that concurrent user intentions need no review. [CRDT definitions and set semantics](https://www.lip6.fr/Marc.Shapiro/papers/2011/Comprehensive-CRDTs-RR7506-2011-01.pdf).
